[b]Markov State Engine [WavesUnchained][/b]

[b]What it does[/b]
The Markov State Engine classifies every bar into one of six market states and measures, from history, where similar states resolved when they ended. It is a regime RESOLUTION map, not a price forecast — the percentages describe how comparable past states resolved, not a prediction of this instance. It is a display of where the market stands: no entry signals, no directional verdicts, and alerts only on state-change events.

[b]The six states[/b]
[list]
[*]Expansion / Chaos and Compression (extreme regimes — tested first)
[*]Up Trend / Down Trend (confirmed trend)
[*]Up Weak / Down Weak (directional context without a confirmed trend)
[/list]
Up/down context comes from price vs EMA and EMA slope (normalized by ATR, so it is scale-free across instruments); "trend" combines ADX strength with path efficiency, so ADX measures strength only, never direction. Compression requires both ATR Rank and an independent BB Width Rank to be low. The state is debounced so brief flicker does not pollute the statistics.

[b]Exit-conditional transition counts[/b]
A first-order chain on sticky regimes is diagonal-dominant — the most likely "next" state is almost always the current one, which just restates the present. So the model is exit-conditional: self-transitions are excluded and reported separately as Persistence, and the remaining exits are aggregated into Exit → Bull / Bear / Neutral. The read is "when this regime breaks, where did it go?"
[list]
[*]Self-transitions excluded; Persistence shows how sticky the state is
[*]Laplace smoothing keeps thin samples from reading a misleading 0% / 100%
[*]The most likely exit destination is shown explicitly
[*]Compression's historical match is further conditioned on a bullish/bearish/neutral flavor, so its exit history is drawn from analogous squeeze setups rather than any Compression
[/list]

[b]Two axes — timing vs direction[/b]
The tool shows two axes that rest on very different sample sizes. TIMING (Persistence and Maturity) is computed from every in-state bar. EXIT DIRECTION (the exit distribution) hangs on the few times the state actually ended, so on sticky states it is statistically thin — the exit count is shown and flagged.
[list]
[*]Maturity = current dwell vs the state's typical dwell (1/(1−Persistence)): Fresh / Mature / Extended / Overdue
[*]"Overdue" is descriptive (unusually long vs history), not a claim that an exit is mechanically due
[*]Below the Minimum Exit Sample the exit distribution and the Resolution Bias line are hidden; only the exit count n is shown
[*]Next to it the base rate: where the exits of all states went over the same lookback
[/list]

[b]Alerts[/b]
All alerts are events, optionally confirmed on bar close:
[list]
[*]MSE State Changed — the debounced state changed
[*]MSE State → Up Trend / Up Weak / Compression / Down Trend / Down Weak / Expansion/Chaos
[*]MSE State Extended — the dwell entered Extended/Overdue
[/list]

[b]Plot & background[/b]
The Resolution Bias line (P(Bull) − P(Bear) of past exits) is a stepline — it only changes value when the state exits, so it holds flat instead of reading as a continuously evolving curve. It is green above zero, red below, and hidden while the exit sample is below the minimum. The pane background tints only Compression/Chaos, with intensity scaling by Maturity — a fresh squeeze is barely tinted, an overdue one reads clearly more saturated.

[b]Readout[/b]
In-pane label: current state and bars in state, a Timing line (Maturity, ratio, typical dwell), a Past exits line (Bull/Bear/Neutral distribution with n and most frequent exit destination), an Any state line (base rate of all exits, with n) and Persistence. Amber only for an Extended/Overdue state, grey otherwise.

